 confusing  microlight global news toronto  another blow to journalism today  buzzfeed which bought huffpost three  weeks ago is laying off 47 employees and  has closed half post canada  and half post quebec visitors to the  site today saw  this as of march 9th huffpost canada  will no longer be publishing content  23 journalists in canada are out of work  buzzfeed says it's part of a  restructuring plan for the whole company  huffpost canada had filed for union  certification in february  the union tweeted today huffpost canada  consistently punched above our weight  and did vital important journalism in  canada  it is a sad day for journalism  the case against the former police  officer accused of murdering george  floyd  coming up jury selection finally begins  this image is capturing lots of  attention a catholic nun  fell to her knees in front of soldiers  in myanmar and asked them to spare the  children and shoot her  instead two of the officers knelt with  her but behind her others  opened fire on protesters violent  crackdowns against demonstrators who  become  a daily occurrence in myanmar more than  60 people have died since the military  seized power in early february including  two people yesterday in the city where  that nun pleaded  for restraint in minneapolis a jury is  now being chosen for the trial of the  former police officer accused of killing  george floyd  the courthouse is heavily fortified  there with concrete barricades  fences and barbed wire and the minnesota  national guard is patrolling the grounds  what happens inside is being closely  watched  as jackson proskow reports even choosing  the jury is expected to take  weeks you have been summoned as  potential jurors  in a trial where everyone has seen the  key piece of evidence  potential jurors were pressed on how  they would decide the fate of former  minneapolis police officer derek shovin  who knelt on the neck of george floyd do  you think that might be a problem  absorbing everything and understanding  all the evidence  i understand but the only i know  what i see in the tv the trial will be  live streamed  jurors will remain anonymous and have  already faced a lengthy questionnaire to  weed out bias  no matter what verdict comes out of this  trial  we want it to be accepted by people on  both sides the death of floyd touched  off national protests and a reckoning  over race and policing in america  in minneapolis floyd's sister is in the  courtroom on behalf of the family  be a man and take action and take and be  responsible for  for what you did man up  you do the crime you do the time chauvin  is charged with second-degree murder and  second-degree manslaughter  prosecutors are trying to add a third  degree murder charge to give the jury  another pathway to convict  all of the jurors in the case have to  agree for there to be  a binding verdict in the case jury  selection could take up to three weeks  the trial could span months  the outcome likely to further divide the  u.s  jackson prosco global news  [Music]  golden comeback ahead the canadian  skiers seeing double  you're watching global national  [Music]  it has been an incredible week for  canadian skier mikhail kingsbury for the  second time in two days he has won gold  at the world freestyle skiing  championships  amazing stuff the reigning olympic  champion from quebec has now won  six world titles and 11 world medals and  even more impressive he only recently  returned to competition after fracturing  two vertebrae  during a training run in december  next reflections from the front lines  there is no way to adequately describe  the last year  none of us has lived through something  like it humans though are remarkably  resilient and yet this pandemic has  tested us  physically mentally and spiritually in  ways we still can't fully comprehend  tonight we have reflections from those  who confront life and death  every day karen lieberman has their  stories  outside downsview long-term care center  in north toronto  the sun is shining spring is near a  season of hope  but it wasn't long ago inside there was  very little  it was sad it's a place i don't want to  go back  plan is updated yes personal support  worker  velvon thomas calls it a dark time  when a severe covid 19 outbreak killed  63 residents  and one staff member you see them today  tomorrow you come  they're gone some you will see have a  little runny nose and a cough  and then you'll just see  the fish change completely change  thinking back triggers a flood of  emotions  you wake up from your sleep crying  you can't barely drive home because  you're crying when more than 100 staff  members were out sick  someone needed to care for the residents  it was like a war zone like honestly  we i don't know what  got us through beneath the shield behind  the mask  lies the trauma the horror of what these  front line health care workers witnessed  everybody went out in a plastic bag  and people think like oh you're used to  death no i'm not  down the street from downsview long-term  care center  at humber river hospital each patient  each loss is etched in the minds of  those who cared for them  we had a young woman with three kids who  before she was intubated  we called the family so she can say  goodbye to her kids  there's no words we cannot say anything  like we just  we just try to be there for them the  shoulder for them to cry on that's the  only thing we can do  in the icu death by kovid can be slow  painful if you have seen a picture of  somebody who is drowning in water  imagine it's lack of oxygen you're  looking for oxygen you see how  distressed that person is going to look  like  a harrowing depiction that will forever  haunt those on the front lines  and sometimes all they could do is be  there  you sing with them you sing for them i  remember one resident  i sing amazing grace how sweet thou art  forever we're gonna have this love and  respect for each other because  of how we just pulled through this  together survived it  yeah it was  a dark time karen lieberman global news  toronto  ryder times are ahead we hope that is  global national for this  tuesday i'm donna friesen tonight's your  canada is buffalo pound provincial park  in southern saskatchewan  please email your canada to viewers at  globalnational.com  thanks for watching hope to see you here  again tomorrow take care of yourselves  [Music]  bye
 

